═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
```

**B. Four-Gate Monitoring Loop**

After generating both handoff prompts, the creating agent polls until ALL FOUR gates clear, then merges: (1) test, (2) review, (3) protected-subitem sweep, (4) branch freshness + mergeability. Between gates 3 and 4 sits an additional CI check-runs allowlist (Gate 3.5) — it requires every check to have reached a positively-safe conclusion (`pass` or `skipping`, per `gh pr checks`'s own `bucket` classification) before merging; a failed/cancelled/timed-out check blocks outright, and a still-running check re-polls rather than passing, so neither a red build nor an unfinished one can slip through under `--admin`.

> **IMPORTANT:** Do NOT check `reviewDecision` — GitHub does not count GitHub App bot reviews toward `required_approving_review_count` on the Team plan. `reviewDecision` will always show `"REVIEW_REQUIRED"` even after both bots approve. Check the reviews list directly, keyed to each bot's own login.

```bash
# ── Seed each gate's last-processed timestamp separately (skip pre-existing reviews) ──
# --paginate is REQUIRED on every call to this endpoint: it pages at 30, so on a
# long-running PR a bot's latest verdict can sit past page 1 and either look
# "not detected", or — worse — let a stale page-1 APPROVED outrank a newer
# CHANGES_REQUESTED once sorted. Never drop --paginate to "simplify" this.
LAST_TEST_AT=$(gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
  --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ds9-tester-bot[bot]")] | sort_by(.submitted_at) | last | .submitted_at // empty')
LAST_REVIEW_AT=$(gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
  --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ai-security-review-bot[bot]")] | sort_by(.submitted_at) | last | .submitted_at // empty')

TEST_OK="false"; REVIEW_OK="false"; MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES=0; NO_CHECKS_RETRIES=0; POLL_COUNT=0; PREV_HEAD_SHA=""; FORCE_PUSH_CUTOFF=""

while true; do
  STATE=$(gh pr view <number> --json state --jq '.state')
  if [ "$STATE" = "CLOSED" ] || [ "$STATE" = "MERGED" ]; then
    echo "PR is $STATE — exiting monitor."
    break
  fi

  POLL_COUNT=$((POLL_COUNT + 1))
  if [ $((POLL_COUNT % 5)) -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Still watching PR #<number>... (poll $POLL_COUNT, TEST=$TEST_OK REVIEW=$REVIEW_OK)"
  fi

  # Key both gates' verdicts to the CURRENT head SHA, not just to a newer timestamp — a
  # review submitted against an older commit must never satisfy a gate for a newer push.
  HEAD_SHA=$(gh pr view <number> --json headRefOid --jq '.headRefOid')

  # TEST_OK/REVIEW_OK are latches — once a poll sets one true it stays true until this
  # loop exits. That's fine for a HEAD that never moves, but if a push lands BETWEEN the
  # two approvals (e.g. the tester approves commit X, then a fix or unrelated push moves
  # HEAD to Y before the reviewer gets to it), the tester's latch stays true forever even
  # though no bot has ever reviewed Y under that flag. The per-poll commit_id filter below
  # only stops a STALE query result from setting a flag true — it does nothing to a flag
  # that is ALREADY true from a prior HEAD. Reset both latches the moment HEAD_SHA itself
  # changes, so every gate must be freshly re-earned against whatever HEAD is current now.
  if [ -n "$PREV_HEAD_SHA" ] && [ "$HEAD_SHA" != "$PREV_HEAD_SHA" ]; then
    echo "Head SHA changed ($PREV_HEAD_SHA -> $HEAD_SHA) since the last poll — a push landed."
    echo "Resetting both gate latches; a prior approval against the old head does not carry"
    echo "forward, each bot must re-approve the new head before this gate re-opens."
    TEST_OK="false"; REVIEW_OK="false"; NO_CHECKS_RETRIES=0

    # ── Force-push / rebase detection (XACA-1159-030) ──────────────────────────────────
    # The committer-date guard below (kept, see its own comment) assumes the NEW head's
    # committer date is newer than any stale review pointed at it. That holds for an
    # ORDINARY rebase — committer dates get rewritten to "now" — but NOT when a force-push
    # re-points HEAD onto a PRE-EXISTING OLDER commit (drop the tip commit and force-push,
    # or rebase with --committer-date-is-author-date): a review submitted after that old
    # commit's original (old) committer date but before it was force-pushed back into place
    # passes the committer-date check even though it never saw this content as HEAD.
    #
    # This is a genuinely different, mechanical check: whether the new head is even a
    # descendant of the previous head at all. An ordinary push is always a fast-forward —
    # PREV_HEAD_SHA remains an ancestor of HEAD_SHA. A force-push/rebase is not: history was
    # rewritten, so PREV_HEAD_SHA is no longer reachable from HEAD_SHA. `compare/A...B`
    # reports `status: "ahead"` exactly when A is an ancestor of B (B is A plus new commits);
    # "diverged" or "behind" means it is not.
    #
    # VALIDATED against this repo's own PR #851 (XACA-1154) HeadRefForcePushedEvent history
    # (`gh api graphql` on the PR's timelineItems), not asserted:
    #   ordinary push   fa1a7598...53b23188 -> compare status="ahead"    (ahead_by=2, behind_by=0)
    #   force-push      53b23188...3c23b5f5 -> compare status="diverged" (ahead_by=8, behind_by=7)
    # (53b23188 was the `beforeCommit` and 3c23b5f5 the `afterCommit` of that PR's third
    # HeadRefForcePushedEvent.) The ordinary push is a clean "ahead"; the force-push is not —
    # exactly the discriminator this check needs.
    #
    # FAILS CLOSED: an unreadable compare result (empty/network error) is treated as a
    # force-push, never as safe.
    #
    # THE ACTUAL MECHANICAL CLOSE: on a detected force-push, stamp the CURRENT wall-clock
    # time as a floor no review can have been submitted before, and require every matching
    # review this poll onward to clear that floor in addition to the commit's own committer
    # date (below). A stale review re-pointed onto the rewritten head by GitHub was, by
    # definition, submitted before this floor was stamped — it cannot pass regardless of
    # what its own submitted_at or the commit's committer date say. A genuinely fresh
    # re-review submitted after this poll clears it normally.
    FF_STATUS=$(gh api "repos/<owner>/<repo>/compare/$PREV_HEAD_SHA...$HEAD_SHA" --jq '.status // empty' 2>/dev/null)
    if [ "$FF_STATUS" != "ahead" ]; then
      echo "⚠ Non-fast-forward change detected (compare status='${FF_STATUS:-<unreadable>}') —"
      echo "  this looks like a force-push/rebase, not an ordinary push. Stamping a fresh"
      echo "  cutoff: no review submitted before this moment can satisfy either gate, no"
      echo "  matter what its commit_id or submitted_at claim."
      FORCE_PUSH_CUTOFF=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    fi
  fi
  PREV_HEAD_SHA="$HEAD_SHA"

  # On an ordinary push, a stale review keeps its OLD commit_id, so the per-poll filter below
  # correctly fails to match it against the new HEAD_SHA and the freshly-reset latch stays
  # false until a real new review lands. On a REBASE, GitHub re-points the commit_id of
  # whatever reviews were current at rebase time onto the NEW head — so a filter that only
  # checks `commit_id == HEAD_SHA` can match a review that predates the rebase and never
  # saw the rebased content, immediately re-setting the latch true on the next poll.
  # Measured, not theoretical: PR #851 (XACA-1154) showed both bots' APPROVED reviews
  # re-pointed onto the post-rebase head 3c23b5f5 within seconds of the force-push, with
  # submitted_at of 13:26:48Z and 13:27:13Z — both BEFORE 3c23b5f5's own committer date of
  # 13:28:41Z (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/commits/3c23b5f5... --jq '.commit.committer.date'`).
  # A head-SHA-keyed APPROVED is proof the review is CURRENT, not proof the reviewer SAW the
  # current content, and those are the same thing only when the head moved by an ordinary push.
  #
  # Fetch the head commit's own committer date and require every matching review's
  # `submitted_at` to be STRICTLY AFTER it (the extra jq clause on Gates 1 and 2 below).
  # A review that predates the commit it is attributed to cannot have seen that commit's
  # content — this is what rejects the re-pointed pair above while still accepting a genuine
  # later re-review on the same head (PR #851 had one, at 13:35:41Z / 13:36:10Z, both after
  # the committer date and correctly accepted). This is a HEURISTIC, not a mechanical
  # guarantee on its own — see the force-push block above for why (it catches an ordinary
  # rebase, whose committer dates get rewritten to "now", but not a force-push onto a
  # pre-existing older commit). The force-push block's wall-clock cutoff above is what
  # actually closes that gap for a rewrite that happens WHILE this loop is running; this
  # date-based check remains as defense-in-depth for a poll that never saw a PREV_HEAD_SHA
  # baseline (the loop's very first poll) or a manual/ad-hoc check reproduced outside it.
  # ISO-8601 `Z` timestamps of equal precision sort correctly as plain strings, so no date
  # parsing is needed — jq's/bash's string `>` does the right thing directly.
  # FAILS CLOSED: if the committer date can't be read, substitute a sentinel date that sorts
  # after every real timestamp, so no review can satisfy either gate until a real committer
  # date is read on a later poll — an unreadable date is never treated as a passing review.
  HEAD_COMMIT_DATE=$(gh api "repos/<owner>/<repo>/commits/$HEAD_SHA" --jq '.commit.committer.date // empty' 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -z "$HEAD_COMMIT_DATE" ]; then
    echo "⚠ could not read committer date for head commit $HEAD_SHA — failing closed: no"
    echo "  review can satisfy either gate this poll until a real committer date is read."
    HEAD_COMMIT_DATE="9999-99-99T99:99:99Z"   # sorts after any real ISO-8601 timestamp
  fi

  # EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF is the LATER of the commit's own committer date and any force-push
  # cutoff stamped above (sticky across polls once set — real wall-clock time only moves
  # forward, so an older force-push's cutoff can never wrongly exceed a newer one). Plain
  # string comparison is valid because both are the same ISO-8601 `Z` format/precision.
  EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF="$HEAD_COMMIT_DATE"
  # `[[ ]]`, NOT `[ ... \> ... ]`. zsh's `[` builtin REJECTS `>` as a string
  # operator -- it errors with "condition expected: >" and the branch is simply
  # not taken, so the force-push floor is never stamped and this silently
  # reverts to the weaker committer-date-only behaviour. It FAILS OPEN, on the
  # shell consumers actually run (zsh is the macOS default). bash accepts the
  # escaped form, which is exactly why testing this under bash alone passes it.
  if [[ -n "$FORCE_PUSH_CUTOFF" && "$FORCE_PUSH_CUTOFF" > "$EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF" ]]; then
    EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF="$FORCE_PUSH_CUTOFF"
  fi

  # ── Gate 1: Test (ds9-tester-bot[bot]) ──
  L=$(gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
    --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ds9-tester-bot[bot]" and .commit_id == "'"$HEAD_SHA"'" and ((.submitted_at // "") > "'"$EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF"'"))] | sort_by(.submitted_at) | last | "\(.submitted_at // "")|\(.state // "")"')
  AT="${L%|*}"; ST="${L#*|}"
  if [ -n "$ST" ] && [ "$AT" != "$LAST_TEST_AT" ]; then
    if [ "$ST" = "CHANGES_REQUESTED" ]; then
      echo "Test failures reported — pausing monitor."
      LAST_TEST_AT="$AT"
      gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
        --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ds9-tester-bot[bot]" and .state == "CHANGES_REQUESTED")] | last | .body'
      # Address feedback, push fixes, re-run the LAST_TEST_AT seed line, re-enter loop.
      break
    elif [ "$ST" = "APPROVED" ]; then
      TEST_OK="true"; LAST_TEST_AT="$AT"; echo "✓ Test gate passed."
    fi
  fi

  # ── Gate 2: Review (ai-security-review-bot[bot]) ──
  L=$(gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
    --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ai-security-review-bot[bot]" and .commit_id == "'"$HEAD_SHA"'" and ((.submitted_at // "") > "'"$EFFECTIVE_CUTOFF"'"))] | sort_by(.submitted_at) | last | "\(.submitted_at // "")|\(.state // "")"')
  AT="${L%|*}"; ST="${L#*|}"
  if [ -n "$ST" ] && [ "$AT" != "$LAST_REVIEW_AT" ]; then
    if [ "$ST" = "CHANGES_REQUESTED" ]; then
      echo "Review changes requested — pausing monitor."
      LAST_REVIEW_AT="$AT"
      gh api --paginate repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number>/reviews \
        --jq '[.[] | select(.user.login == "ai-security-review-bot[bot]" and .state == "CHANGES_REQUESTED")] | last | .body'
      # Address feedback, push fixes, re-run the LAST_REVIEW_AT seed line, re-enter loop.
      break
    elif [ "$ST" = "APPROVED" ]; then
      REVIEW_OK="true"; LAST_REVIEW_AT="$AT"; echo "✓ Review gate passed."
    fi
  fi

  if [ "$TEST_OK" != "true" ] || [ "$REVIEW_OK" != "true" ]; then
    sleep 60
    continue
  fi

  # ── Gate 3: Protected subitem sweep ──
  # Gate on the literal marker in kb-sweep's OUTPUT — never its exit code (that
  # over-blocks on framework subitems that legitimately complete AFTER merge), and
  # never a homegrown board.json re-parse (a bypass that has merged a PR through an
  # open [Review] subitem before).
  #
  # ABSENCE OF THE MARKER IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A CLEAN SWEEP (XACA-1159-033): checking
  # only "does the output contain PROTECTED SUBITEMS UNRESOLVED" proves the bad text
  # isn't there — it says nothing about whether kb-sweep ever ran. A failed `source`
  # leaves the `kb-sweep` shell function undefined; the next line then errors with
  # something like "kb-sweep: command not found" (bash) or "command not found:
  # kb-sweep" (zsh) and returns non-zero — output that plainly contains no marker
  # text, which the old check read as a clean sweep. Same shape as XACA-1145's shipped
  # kb-sweep, which had NO protected-subitem gate at all and failed open on every
  # consumer box: a check that never runs and a check that never matches are
  # indistinguishable from a passing one.
  #
  # Require POSITIVE evidence the sweep actually completed instead. kb-sweep always
  # prints a "SUBITEM STATUS SWEEP: <id>" header right after it resolves the item,
  # followed by either "No subitems found — ready to close." (zero-subitem items) or
  # the "Legend:" line that only prints after the subitem table has been walked (items
  # with subitems) — see kanban-helpers.sh. Neither line appears on any error path (bad
  # context, item not found, undefined function). FAILS CLOSED: no header+completion
  # pair found -> block, even though the PROTECTED SUBITEMS marker is also absent.
  source {{AITEAMFORGE_DIR}}/kanban-helpers.sh
  SOURCE_RC=$?
  SWEEP_OUTPUT=$(kb-sweep <ITEM-ID> 2>&1)
  SWEEP_RC=$?
  echo "$SWEEP_OUTPUT"
  SWEEP_RAN_OK="false"
  if echo "$SWEEP_OUTPUT" | grep -qE "SUBITEM STATUS SWEEP: <ITEM-ID>" \
     && echo "$SWEEP_OUTPUT" | grep -qE "ready to close\.|^  Legend:"; then
    SWEEP_RAN_OK="true"
  fi
  if [ "$SOURCE_RC" -ne 0 ] || [ "$SWEEP_RAN_OK" != "true" ]; then
    echo "✗ kb-sweep did NOT positively confirm a completed sweep for <ITEM-ID>"
    echo "  (source rc=$SOURCE_RC, kb-sweep rc=$SWEEP_RC). No 'SUBITEM STATUS SWEEP:"
    echo "  <ITEM-ID>' header + completion line found in its output — the PROTECTED"
    echo "  SUBITEMS marker being absent does NOT mean the sweep ran clean; it may not"
    echo "  have run at all. Fix the source/invocation (check kanban-helpers.sh path,"
    echo "  team context) before treating this gate as passed. Not merging."
    break
  fi
  # kb-sweep's OWN exit code (SWEEP_RC) is deliberately NOT part of the pass/fail
  # decision above or below — it is legitimately non-zero for reasons unrelated to
  # protected subitems (e.g. a framework subitem that completes AFTER merge), which is
  # exactly the documented over-blocking this gate must not reintroduce. It is captured
  # only to make the diagnostic above concrete.
  if echo "$SWEEP_OUTPUT" | grep -qE "PROTECTED SUBITEMS UNRESOLVED \([0-9]+\)"; then
    echo "✗ Protected [Review]/[Test]/[UX] subitems are still open — agents cannot cancel"
    echo "  them without user approval. Resolve them, push fixes if needed, re-enter the loop."
    break
  fi

  # ── Gate 3.5: CI check runs — allowlist of positively-safe conclusions, not a denylist
  #    of one bad state ──
  # `--admin` bypasses branch-protection review counting, NOT CI results — a build/lint/test
  # workflow that has not positively concluded safely must still block the merge.
  #
  # NOTE THE IRONY THIS REPLACES: the prior version of this gate keyed only on
  # `state == "FAILURE"`, in a comment that itself warned against enumerated denylists —
  # while doing exactly that with a single enumerated bad value. `state == "FAILURE"` cannot
  # distinguish "hasn't failed YET (still running)" from "actually passed", and it does not
  # match CANCELLED, TIMED_OUT, ACTION_REQUIRED, or STALE — none of those equal the string
  # "FAILURE", so all of them sailed through under the old check. Measured on this repo's own
  # PR #855: `shell-suite` alone took 5m38s (19:36:29Z -> 19:42:07Z) while the gate bots do
  # not wait for GitHub CI to finish — a real, not hypothetical, unfinished-check race.
  #
  # `gh pr checks --json` exposes a `bucket` field precisely for this: gh's own source
  # (pkg/cmd/pr/checks/aggregate.go, verified against upstream, not assumed) maps every
  # check state/conclusion into exactly one of five buckets:
  #   pass     = SUCCESS
  #   skipping = SKIPPED, NEUTRAL
  #   fail     = ERROR, FAILURE, TIMED_OUT, ACTION_REQUIRED
  #   cancel   = CANCELLED
  #   pending  = everything else (QUEUED, IN_PROGRESS, WAITING, EXPECTED, REQUESTED, STALE,
  #              and any future/unrecognised state — this bucket is gh's own default case)
  # Require every check to be `pass` or `skipping` before merging. `fail` or `cancel` blocks
  # outright. Anything else — including a state gh doesn't recognise yet — lands in `pending`
  # by construction and is treated as "not finished", which re-polls rather than merges; an
  # unrecognised state is never treated as safe.
  #
  # ⚠️ VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED: `gh pr checks --help` documents an "Additional exit codes: 8:
  # Checks pending" — but that exit code is produced only by the plain-text output path; in
  # `--json` mode (which this gate uses) the command writes JSON and returns success
  # regardless of pending checks (confirmed against gh's own source, pkg/cmd/pr/checks/checks.go).
  # Do NOT rely on `gh pr checks`'s own exit code here — inspect the `bucket` field values
  # directly, exactly as this gate does.
  #
  # A PR with NO checks configured at all is a distinct case from an unreadable/failed API
  # call: `gh pr checks` errors with "no checks reported on the '<branch>' branch" when the
  # check list is genuinely empty (verified against gh's source), which is not a reason to
  # block forever. Any OTHER read failure fails closed — an unreadable answer is never
  # evidence of safety.
  #
  # "NO CHECKS REPORTED" IS ITSELF AMBIGUOUS (XACA-1159-034): gh prints this exact string
  # in TWO genuinely different situations that look identical from one read — (a) this
  # repo/branch has no CI configured at all, and (b) GitHub has not finished REGISTERING
  # the checks that a just-landed push WILL trigger yet, a transient state that lasts a
  # handful of seconds. Accepting (a)'s conclusion ("nothing to gate on, proceed") the
  # first time this string appears means a PR can merge in the gap of case (b), before CI
  # even exists to gate on. Neither case is distinguishable from a single read.
  #
  # Resolve it the same way Gate 4 resolves `mergeable=UNKNOWN` (an analogous GitHub
  # eventual-consistency gap): bound a re-poll against the SAME head SHA rather than
  # guessing on the first answer. NO_CHECKS_RETRIES counts consecutive "no checks
  # reported" reads while HEAD_SHA is unchanged; it resets to 0 on a new push (a fresh
  # head earns a fresh grace period — see the HEAD_SHA-change block above) and again
  # below the moment `gh pr checks` reports real check data (case (a) is now
  # unambiguously ruled out). Below the threshold this re-polls exactly like the
  # PENDING_CHECKS branch two paragraphs down; only once the ambiguity has had multiple
  # polls to resolve and STILL reads "no checks reported" is it accepted as case (a).
  # Bounded, not indefinite: worst case is 5 * 10s = 50s, mirroring Gate 4's own
  # MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES bound rather than inventing a new shape for the same problem.
  CHECKS_OUT=$(gh pr checks <number> --repo <owner>/<repo> --json name,bucket \
    --jq '.[] | "\(.name)|\(.bucket)"' 2>&1)
  CHECKS_RC=$?
  if [ $CHECKS_RC -ne 0 ]; then
    if echo "$CHECKS_OUT" | grep -qi "no checks reported"; then
      NO_CHECKS_RETRIES=$((NO_CHECKS_RETRIES + 1))
      if [ "$NO_CHECKS_RETRIES" -lt 5 ]; then
        echo "gh reports 'no checks reported' for PR #<number> at $HEAD_SHA (poll"
        echo "  $NO_CHECKS_RETRIES/5 since this head appeared) — could mean no CI is"
        echo "  configured, or that GitHub hasn't registered checks for this push yet."
        echo "  Not assuming either; re-polling before accepting this as a genuine pass."
        sleep 10
        continue
      fi
      echo "'no checks reported' persisted for $NO_CHECKS_RETRIES consecutive polls"
      echo "  against the same head ($HEAD_SHA) — treating as genuinely no CI configured"
      echo "  on this repo/branch, proceeding."
    else
      echo "✗ Could not read CI check status for PR #<number> (gh pr checks exited $CHECKS_RC):"
      echo "$CHECKS_OUT"
      echo "  Not assuming safe — investigate before merging."
      break
    fi
  else
    NO_CHECKS_RETRIES=0   # real check data observed — case (a) is now unambiguously ruled out
    UNSAFE_CHECKS=""
    PENDING_CHECKS=""
    # A here-doc, NOT a pipe: a `while` on the right of a pipe runs in a subshell and the
    # accumulator variables would not survive the loop.
    while IFS='|' read -r C_NAME C_BUCKET; do
      [ -n "$C_NAME" ] || continue
      case "$C_BUCKET" in
        pass|skipping) : ;;
        fail|cancel) UNSAFE_CHECKS="${UNSAFE_CHECKS}${UNSAFE_CHECKS:+, }$C_NAME ($C_BUCKET)" ;;
        *) PENDING_CHECKS="${PENDING_CHECKS}${PENDING_CHECKS:+, }$C_NAME (${C_BUCKET:-<unreadable>})" ;;
      esac
    done <<CHECKS_EOF
$CHECKS_OUT
CHECKS_EOF
    if [ -n "$UNSAFE_CHECKS" ]; then
      echo "✗ Failing/cancelled/timed-out check(s) on PR #<number>: $UNSAFE_CHECKS"
      echo "  Investigate before merging. Do NOT --admin past this — --admin bypasses the"
      echo "  Team-plan review-count block, it is not a license to merge over a failed CI run."
      break
    fi
    if [ -n "$PENDING_CHECKS" ]; then
      echo "CI still running on PR #<number>: $PENDING_CHECKS — re-polling rather than merging"
      echo "  on an unfinished build."
      sleep 60
      continue
    fi
  fi

  # ── Gate 4: Branch freshness + mergeability ──
  # Order matters. Check UNKNOWN FIRST, not last: GitHub computes `mergeable` and
  # `mergeStateStatus` asynchronously, so either can read UNKNOWN for a moment right
  # after a push. Testing for CONFLICTING while a field is still UNKNOWN makes that
  # test false and lets a genuinely conflicted PR sail through.
  MERGE_INFO=$(gh pr view <number> --json mergeable,mergeStateStatus --jq '"\(.mergeable)|\(.mergeStateStatus)"')
  MERGEABLE="${MERGE_INFO%|*}"; MERGE_STATE="${MERGE_INFO#*|}"

  if [ -z "$MERGEABLE" ] || [ -z "$MERGE_STATE" ] || [ "$MERGEABLE" = "UNKNOWN" ] || [ "$MERGE_STATE" = "UNKNOWN" ]; then
    MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES=$((MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES + 1))
    if [ "$MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES" -ge 5 ]; then
      echo "✗ Mergeability stuck at UNKNOWN after 5 polls — not safe to assume mergeable. Pausing for a human look."
      break
    fi
    echo "Mergeability still UNKNOWN (poll $MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES/5) — re-polling rather than proceeding."
    sleep 10
    continue
  fi
  MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES=0

  # A content conflict needs manual resolution — `gh pr update-branch` fixes staleness,
  # never content conflicts. Never merge over this.
  if [ "$MERGEABLE" = "CONFLICTING" ] || [ "$MERGE_STATE" = "DIRTY" ]; then
    echo "✗ PR #<number> has merge conflicts (mergeable=$MERGEABLE, mergeStateStatus=$MERGE_STATE)."
    echo "  Resolve manually: checkout the branch, merge/rebase the base branch in, resolve"
    echo "  conflicts, push, then re-spawn BOTH bots, re-seed both LAST_*_AT, re-enter the loop."
    break
  fi

  # Count staleness via the compare API's `behind_by` — never read it off
  # `mergeStateStatus`. That field reports exactly one state, and a PR that's
  # bot-approved-but-unmet-by-branch-protection can sit at BLOCKED permanently on a
  # Team plan, which MASKS a genuine BEHIND state entirely. Fail closed if the count
  # can't be read — an unreadable answer is not evidence of freshness.
  BASE_REF=$(gh pr view <number> --json baseRefName --jq '.baseRefName')
  BEHIND_BY=$(gh api "repos/<owner>/<repo>/compare/$BASE_REF...$HEAD_SHA" --jq '.behind_by' 2>/dev/null)
  case "$BEHIND_BY" in ''|*[!0-9]*) BEHIND_BY="" ;; esac
  if [ -z "$BEHIND_BY" ]; then
    echo "✗ Could not determine how far behind $BASE_REF this PR is — not assuming fresh. Investigate before merging."
    break
  fi
  if [ "$BEHIND_BY" -gt 0 ] || [ "$MERGE_STATE" = "BEHIND" ]; then
    echo "Branch is $BEHIND_BY commit(s) behind $BASE_REF — running gh pr update-branch."
    echo "Prior bot approvals are against a stale base; re-spawn BOTH bots, re-seed both"
    echo "LAST_*_AT, re-enter the loop after this."
    gh pr update-branch <number>
    break
  fi

  # Allowlist, not denylist: `mergeStateStatus` is a vocabulary (CLEAN/BEHIND/DIRTY/
  # BLOCKED/UNSTABLE/DRAFT/UNKNOWN), not a boolean — merge only on states verified safe
  # here. BLOCKED is the normal steady state on a Team plan (bot reviews don't count
  # toward the required-approval count), NOT an error — it carries no readiness signal
  # in either direction, which is exactly why the merge below needs --admin.
  if [ "$MERGE_STATE" != "CLEAN" ] && [ "$MERGE_STATE" != "UNSTABLE" ] && [ "$MERGE_STATE" != "BLOCKED" ]; then
    echo "✗ mergeStateStatus=$MERGE_STATE is not on the merge allowlist (CLEAN, UNSTABLE, BLOCKED) — investigate before merging."
    break
  fi

  echo "All gates passed + branch fresh (mergeable=$MERGEABLE, mergeStateStatus=$MERGE_STATE). Merging with --admin..."
  gh pr merge <number> --squash --delete-branch --admin
  kb-done
  break
done
```

**Monitoring Behavior:**
- Poll every **60 seconds**; a brief status line every 5 polls (~5 min) shows TEST/REVIEW state.
- Both gates key their verdict to the PR's **current head SHA** (`commit_id`) — an approval from before an ordinary push can't satisfy a gate for the new one. **Head-SHA keying alone is NOT sufficient after a rebase or force-push**: GitHub re-points an existing review's `commit_id` onto the new head, so the same filter can match a review that predates the rebase and never saw the rebased content (measured on PR #851 — both bots' `commit_id` re-pointed within seconds of the force-push, with `submitted_at` timestamps *before* that commit's own committer date). Two layered checks close this, with different guarantees — **state the residual honestly rather than calling the combination bulletproof**:
  - **Mechanically enforced** for a force-push/rebase that happens *while this loop instance is running*: each poll compares the new head against the previous poll's head via the compare API (`compare/PREV...NEW`); anything other than `status: "ahead"` (fast-forward) is treated as a rewrite regardless of what any review's `commit_id` or `submitted_at` claims, and stamps the current wall-clock time as a floor no review can have been submitted before. Fails closed if the compare call is unreadable. Verified against this repo's own PR #851 `HeadRefForcePushedEvent` history: an ordinary push compared `ahead` (2/0), the actual force-push compared `diverged` (8/7).
  - **Heuristic, not mechanical**, for a rewrite that already happened *before the loop's first poll* (no previous-head baseline to compare against) or a manual/ad-hoc check outside the loop: falls back to requiring each matching review's `submitted_at` to be strictly after the head commit's own `commit.committer.date`. This catches an ordinary rebase (committer dates get rewritten to "now") but is known to miss a force-push that re-points HEAD onto a pre-existing *older* commit, since a stale review's `submitted_at` can still be later than that old commit's own committer date. That residual case still depends on the operator's judgment — treat any rebase/force-push as a fresh review round, re-spawn both bots, re-seed both `LAST_*_AT`, as a matter of course.
  See the inline comments above Gate 1 and Troubleshooting § "rebase re-points commit_id" for the measured detail and the manual verification query.
- On `CHANGES_REQUESTED` from either bot: pause, surface the review body, address feedback, push fixes, re-seed ONLY that gate's `LAST_*_AT`, re-enter the loop.
- Gate 3 (subitem sweep), Gate 3.5 (CI check runs), and Gate 4 (freshness/mergeability) only run once BOTH bot gates have passed — see the loop's ordering above.
- **Gate 3 requires POSITIVE evidence kb-sweep ran**, not merely the absence of the `PROTECTED SUBITEMS UNRESOLVED` marker: a failed `source` of kanban-helpers.sh (or any other error that stops kb-sweep from running) also produces output without that marker, and the old check read that silence as a clean sweep. The gate now requires the `SUBITEM STATUS SWEEP: <id>` header plus a completion line (`ready to close.` or `Legend:`) before it will trust the marker's absence at all — missing either fails closed, regardless of what the marker check would have said.
- Gate 3.5 is an **allowlist**, not a denylist of one bad state: every check must land in gh's own `pass` or `skipping` bucket before merging — `fail` (FAILURE/ERROR/TIMED_OUT/ACTION_REQUIRED) or `cancel` (CANCELLED) blocks outright, and anything still `pending` (including a state gh doesn't recognise) re-polls rather than passing. `--admin` overrides the Team-plan review-count requirement, it does not and must not override a red, cancelled, or still-running CI run. `gh pr checks`'s own exit-code-8-on-pending behavior is documented for plain-text output only — in `--json` mode (used here) it returns success regardless of pending checks, so this gate inspects the `bucket` field directly rather than trusting the command's exit code.
- **`gh pr checks`'s "no checks reported" is ambiguous, not a clean pass on first read**: it means either "no CI configured" or "GitHub hasn't registered this push's checks yet" — indistinguishable from one query. The gate re-polls (`NO_CHECKS_RETRIES`, bounded at 5 polls / 10s each, same shape as Gate 4's `MERGE_UNKNOWN_RETRIES`) against the unchanged head SHA before accepting it as a genuine "no CI here"; the counter resets on a new push and the moment real check data is observed.
- On a stale branch (Gate 4): `gh pr update-branch` dismisses stale reviews — re-spawn BOTH bots and re-seed BOTH timestamps afterward.
- On merge conflicts (Gate 4): halt, never merge — resolve manually, then re-spawn both bots.
- `state: "CLOSED"` or `"MERGED"` → notify the user and exit the monitor.
- **NEVER** use `echo "$VAR" | jq` — control characters break parsing; always use `gh api --jq`.
- Uses `--admin` because the GitHub Team plan does not count bot reviews toward branch protection — `reviewDecision` always reads `REVIEW_REQUIRED` even after both approve.
- **Do NOT** merge without all four gates clearing — the monitoring loop handles merge automatically.

